 ],
 "text": "Your life has never been this comfortable. Food arrives in twenty minutes. Every show ever made is one tap away. The temperature is always perfect. So why does everyone feel so tired? Here's the trap. Comfort isn't free. Every bit of friction you remove from your day was also a small workout for your will. Walk less, and your legs get weaker. Choose less, and your judgment does too. Two thousand years ago, Seneca wrote that we suffer more often in imagination than in reality. We used to meet small discomforts every day, and they kept our fears honest. Now the smallest inconvenience feels like an emergency. So here's the move. Put friction back on purpose. Take the stairs. Cook the meal. Sit with boredom for ten minutes before you reach for your phone. Not because suffering is good, but because a little resistance keeps you strong enough to handle the real thing. Comfort is a tool. Don't let it become the room you live in.",
 "speech_model_used": "script+pocketsphinx-align",
 "source": "local"
